---

# 5. Dashboard Layout

The full dashboard should use a visual hierarchy based on:

```text
GLOBAL USAGE
      ↓
ACTIVE SESSIONS
      ↓
SESSION DETAILS
      ↓
HISTORY / ANALYTICS
```

---

# 6. Global Usage Visualization

Provide multiple time windows if available from the underlying usage source.

Example:

```text
5 HOUR WINDOW

████████████████░░░░
72% used

Remaining
28%

Reset
2h 14m
```

And:

```text
7 DAY WINDOW

███████░░░░░░░░░░░
34% used
```

The user should be able to switch:

```text
5h | 7d
```

Do not display fake quota information.

If the data source cannot reliably provide a metric:

```text
Usage unavailable
```

instead of inventing a value.

---

# 7. Energy Ring

For the full dashboard, optionally use an energy ring.

Example:

```text
          ╭─────────╮
       ╭──┘         └──╮
      │      72%        │
      │                 │
       ╰──╮         ╭──╯
          ╰─────────╯

       Claude Usage
```

The ring should animate subtly when usage changes.

Avoid aggressive animations.

---

# 8. Active Session Visualization

Each active Claude Code session should appear as a compact "energy cell".

Example:

```text
┌────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ ● DA-YO                         ACTIVE │
│                                        │
│ ~/Projects/DA-YO                     │
│                                        │
│ Editing                                │
│ src/components/Menu.tsx               │
│                                        │
│ Token Energy                           │
│ █████████████░░░░░  42.8k             │
│                                        │
│ 12m 34s                     12.4k/min  │
└────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Do not show every field by default.

Priority:

1. Status
2. Project
3. Current activity
4. Token usage
5. Duration
6. Token rate

---

# 9. Session Status

Support these states:

```text
RUNNING
IDLE
WAITING
PERMISSION
ERROR
COMPLETED
```

Example:

```text
● Working
○ Idle
◌ Waiting
! Permission required
× Error
✓ Completed
```

Use animation only for active states.

---

# 10. "What is Claude Doing?"

This is one of the most important features.

The UI should translate low-level activity into human-readable descriptions.

Examples:

```text
Reading package.json
Editing Menu.tsx
Running npm test
Installing dependencies
Searching codebase
Running git status
Waiting for input
Waiting for permission
```

Do not simply display raw terminal output.

Prefer:

```text
Running tests
```

over:

```text
npm run test -- --coverage
```

The raw command can be available as secondary detail.

---

# 11. Token Visualization

Do not display tokens only as numbers.

Use a compact energy bar:

```text
Token Energy

██████████████░░░░
42.8k tokens
```

And optionally:

```text
Input      21.4k
Output      8.2k
Cache      13.2k
Total      42.8k
```

Use human-readable formatting:

```text
1,200 → 1.2k
12,400 → 12.4k
128,000 → 128k
1,240,000 → 1.24M
```

---

# 12. Token Burn Rate

Add:

```text
12.4k tokens/min
```

This lets the user understand whether a session is consuming tokens unusually quickly.

Visualize it as a small sparkline:

```text
Token Rate

▁▂▃▅▆▅▇█▆▅
12.4k/min
```

The sparkline should be extremely subtle.

---

# 13. Context Usage

If context window information is available:

```text
Context

██████████████░░░░
142k / 200k

71%
```

Use warning states:

```text
< 70%   Healthy
70–85%  Attention
85–95%  Warning
> 95%   Critical
```

These thresholds should be configurable constants.

Do not claim a context limit unless the model/source provides it.

---

# 14. Cost Tracking

Provide estimated cost only when token/model pricing is known.

Example:

```text
Today

438k tokens
$3.42 estimated
```

Session:

```text
DA-YO
42.8k tokens
$0.34 estimated
```

Clearly label this as:

```text
Estimated
```

Never present an estimate as an actual billing amount.

---

